course: dictionary
lesson: 9875
title: What is a tabloid?
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: A tabloid is a kind of newspaper or magazine. Its pages are smaller, it has lots of photos, and its stories are short and eye-catching. The context tells you what it is about: «A tabloid is a newspaper or magazine that sells by shocking headlines and gossip about famous people.» Shocking and gossip are the clues.
explain: Tabloid can also describe news. When we say «tabloid news», we mean news that is sensational, shocking or gossipy. Ask yourself: what word would fit here? Then try your meaning in the sentence. Does «tabloid news» still make sense if you think of it as shocking gossip? It does, so your meaning is probably right.
explain: In media lessons you learn to compare a tabloid with a broadsheet, which is a bigger kind of newspaper. You also learn to check a headline. A tabloid headline may be exaggerated, which means made to sound bigger or more dramatic than the facts. One ordinary photo plus dramatic words can become a whole story, so always ask: is this true?
fact: A tabloid is a newspaper or magazine with smaller pages, many photos and short, eye-catching stories.
fact: «Tabloid» can also be an adjective: «tabloid news» means sensational, shocking or gossipy news.
fact: Tabloids often write about famous people, crime and gossip.
fact: A tabloid is smaller than a broadsheet, which is a bigger size of newspaper page.
fact: «Tabloid headlines» can be exaggerated, so check whether they are true before you believe them.
exercise: «A tabloid is a newspaper or magazine that sells by shocking headlines and gossip about famous people.» What is a tabloid? | *A newspaper or magazine with shocking headlines and gossip | A very large book about famous places | A kind of phone app for maps | A paper used for wrapping fish
exercise: Tabloid news is a phrase you may hear. What does tabloid mean when it describes news? | Calm, careful and fully checked | *Sensational and gossipy | Written only by children | Very old and valuable
exercise: Find and copy one word from «a newspaper or magazine that sells by shocking headlines and gossip about famous people» that means talk about other people's private lives. | headlines | famous | newspaper | *gossip
test: Tabloids have smaller pages than which kind of newspaper? | Scroll | Pamphlet | Poster | *Broadsheet
test: In a media lesson, what should you check about a tabloid headline? | *Whether it is true or exaggerated | Whether it is printed in colour | Whether it is the longest story in the paper
exam: Ella reads a tabloid headline that says «Star's shock secret!» What should she check first? | Whether the photo is in colour | Whether the paper is heavy to carry | Whether the page is too big for her bag | *Whether the headline is true or exaggerated
